Technique 2:For incremental load(files)
---------------------------------------
FLOW:USING ARCHEIVE 
[Get Metadata: list files in landing/]
        ↓
[ForEach file]
    ├─ [Copy #1: landing/{file} → staging/Synapse]
    ├─ [Copy #2: landing/{file} → archive/{file}]
    ├─ [Delete: landing/{file}]                          ← only after both copies succeed
    └─ [Databricks Notebook: base parameters = file_name (@item().name), run_id (@pipeline().RunId)]

"Get Metadata activity, with that we get details of file" — Correct. It lists the files currently sitting in the landing folder (Child Items).

"After that, ForEach activity, inside I use 2 Copy activities, one is to pull data from landing zone and other one is to archive zone" — Correct. Both Copy activities read the source from landing/{file}:

Copy #1: landing/{file} → staging/Synapse (the real data load).
Copy #2: landing/{file} → archive/{file} (the backup/history copy).

"After that I add Delete activity for the landing zone" — Correct. Delete removes the file from landing/ only, and this should run after both Copy activities succeed (connected via green/success dependencies), so you never delete a file that wasn't fully processed and backed up yet.

"So every time we get new files only" — Correct. Since processed files are removed from landing, whatever Get Metadata lists next run is automatically only the new, unprocessed ones. No comparison logic needed.

"Whenever we run pipeline, we give pipeline name/id, with data to process that file alone, until the end of pipeline, passing through notebook base parameters" — Correct. Inside the ForEach, @item().name is the current file, and you pass it (along with @pipeline().RunId if useful for traceability) into the Databricks Notebook activity's Base parameters, so the notebook processes exactly that one file, and every step downstream can be traced back to a specific file and a specific pipeline run.

